 'lesson:enterprise-network-segmentation':'Navigation container separates packet forwarding, policy enforcement, admission and delivery. Its nine selected children qualify as bounded drafts, without owner approval or microsegmentation/zero-trust implementation coverage.',
 'keywords:enterprise-network-segmentation':'Three bounded definitions distinguish policy-oriented zones, a platform-dependent access list and minimal time-bounded identity permissions. They are vocabulary rather than executed controls.',
 'traps:enterprise-network-segmentation':'VLAN boundaries need routing/filter enforcement; ACL order matters under first-match semantics; load balancing and TLS termination do not imply full security inspection.',
 'check:enterprise-network-segmentation':'The stated earlier broad permit plausibly shadows the later deny in a first-match ACL. The revealed answer names that premise and narrowing/reordering; no packet evaluation or universal ACL semantics is credited.',
 'section:enterprise-network-segmentation:0':'VLAN layer-2 domains differ from IP subnets/routed boundaries. DMZ, east-west/north-south and air-gap labels require actual restricted paths; media, maintenance, radio and people can bridge isolation.',
 'section:enterprise-network-segmentation:1':'Rule direction/endpoints/protocol/port/state/action and platform evaluation determine access. First-match shadowing and implicit deny are explicitly conditional; connection state does not imply least privilege.',
 'section:enterprise-network-segmentation:2':'The four complete rows distinguish identity/posture admission, spoofable MAC port restriction, health-based connection delivery and approved TLS decryption with downstream trust/protection duties.',
 'lesson:cloud-containers-automation':'Partial navigation container for cloud service/deployment models, container isolation, automation and serverless. The deployment-model section and three generated checks remain held; accurate sections do not repair them.',
 'keywords:cloud-containers-automation':'Partial vocabulary guide: responsibility split and approved maintained baseline entries qualify as bounded drafts. The IaC entry remains held because its unqualified reviewed-file wording conflates a separate review control with the defining mechanism.',
 'traps:cloud-containers-automation':'Service/contract-specific duties, normally shared container kernel, customer serverless code/configuration work and scalable insecure IaC distinguish operational benefits from security guarantees.',
 'check:cloud-containers-automation':'Repeated deployment scales an already reviewed configuration error; checks/staging/least privilege are preventive goals, not guaranteed success. This ungraded reveal does not teach review as sufficient for safety.',
 'section:cloud-containers-automation:0':'Three commonly-qualified responsibility rows distinguish IaaS guest layers, PaaS application/configuration layers and SaaS tenant/access/data duties from underlying provider operation.',
 'section:cloud-containers-automation:1':'HOLD: the hybrid-cloud row permits merely coordinated distinct environments without establishing distinct cloud infrastructures and technology-enabled data/application portability. The complete five-row section receives zero credit; no independent prior hybrid hold is resolved.',
 'section:cloud-containers-automation:2':'VMs usually have a guest OS; containers commonly share the host kernel with OS process/resource isolation. Privilege/kernel/orchestrator/image exposure can cross boundaries; whole-chain controls and timely rebuilds do not prove runtime safety.',
 'section:cloud-containers-automation:3':'SDN separates control decisions from forwarding with implementation variation. IaC makes intended state reviewable/repeatable but scales mistakes; explicit repository/review/policy/environment/credential/secret/rollout/rollback/drift controls remain necessary. Replacement-based immutability needs deliberate data/evidence handling.',
 'section:cloud-containers-automation:4':'Serverless shifts underlying server operation while customer code/dependency/identity/trigger/secret/data/network/log/runtime choices persist. SOA and microservices increase ownership/boundary complexity without intrinsic security.'
